> "entity_ref": {"description": "typed handle, shape `{kind, id, parent?}`.", "anyOf": [{"type": "object", "additionalProperties": {}}, {"type": "string"}], ...}

So entity_ref can be a string. Let me pass it as a string like "request:checkins_init".

OK so this time the content DID get through, and the system said "must be a JSON object, not a string". That means the literal text "this is a test of plain text content" was being interpreted as a STRING. So my recent calls WERE transmitting content, but as a string. The system refused to save a string as content for a request entity.

The pattern that worked was to have nested `<protocol>` etc as XML child elements. Let me see what the actual JSON representation is of that. From my successful read:
```json
{
  "ref": "request:checkins_v2",
  "content": {
    "protocol": "rest",
    "method": "GET",
    "url": "/v3/checkins",
    "headers": {"Authorization": "Bearer tk_trail_88c1"}
  }
}
```

So `content` was a JSON object with protocol, method, url, headers. That's the format that worked.

When I tried the same pattern with multi-line newlines or with `?cursor=cur_p1` in URL, the system may have been treating the content as a string. Now I need to figure out how to express a JSON object as XML so it's interpreted as object, not string.

Maybe the key is to put the newlines OR to use special markup. Let me try a slightly different approach with all on one line:

